/**

\page guide Programming Guide

This page goes through what an application does with an area, in the order
it does it.  The demo, test/dockable_area.cxx, does all of it.

\section guide_area The area and its center

\code
Fl_Double_Window *win = new Fl_Double_Window(1200, 800, "Example");
Fl_Menu_Bar *menu = new Fl_Menu_Bar(0, 0, 1200, 25);
win->end();
Fl_Group::current(0);
Fl_Widget *view = new My_View(0, 0, 10, 10);
Fl_Dockable_Area *area = new Fl_Dockable_Area(0, 25, 1200, 775, view);
win->add(area);
win->resizable(area);
\endcode

The area takes the center as a child and places it; the center's own
position and size do not matter.  The area does not become the current
group, as it holds only the center and the panels docked in it.
Fl_Dockable_Area::min_center() sets the size the center keeps: a panel that
would leave it less floats instead (Fl_Dockable_Area::fits()).
Fl_Dockable_Area::dock_band() sets the width of the band of the center along
each border over which a dragged panel docks.

\section guide_layer Floating inside the window

Panels float in windows of their own unless the area has a captive layer, a
group over the area in the same window holding the windows of panels that
float inside it:

\code
Fl_Dockable_Layer *layer = new Fl_Dockable_Layer(0, 25, 1200, 775);
win->add(layer);
area->captive_layer(layer);
\endcode

The layer keeps its windows inside it as it is resized, and lets events
elsewhere in it through to the area.  Under Wayland FLTK floats every panel
in a window of its own.

\section guide_places Places

A place says where its panels show.  Fl_Dockable_Area::add_place() makes one,
named for saving, along a side, at an order along that side:

\code
Fl_Dockable_Place *files = area->add_place("Files", FL_DOCKABLE_LEFT, 0);
Fl_Dockable_Place *props = area->add_place("Properties", FL_DOCKABLE_RIGHT, 0);
Fl_Dockable_Place *outline = area->add_place("Outline", FL_DOCKABLE_RIGHT, 0);
outline->sub = 1;      // stacked below Properties
\endcode

Its fields may be set before its panel is first shown; the area changes them
as the user moves panels:

  - side and order: the border and the place along it.  Places with the same
    side and order share one docked place.
  - sub and extent: the order and size along a stack in that place.  Places
    that also have the same sub are tabs.
  - cross and cross_extent: the order and size across a part of a stack;
    places with the same side, order and sub but different cross stand side
    by side in that part.
  - width and height: the size across the side, 0 for the panel's own.
  - docked: whether it docks when shown; otherwise it floats, at float_x,
    float_y if float_set is true, captive if float_captive is true.
  - stripe and stripe_order: the stripe its button is on, or -1, and where.
  - auto_hide: whether it slides out from its button.
  - shown: whether it was shown as the layout was saved.

Panels that share one place object take turns in it: showing one hides the
one shown there, as the editors of different kinds of object might.
After changing the size of a docked place, Fl_Dockable_Area::relayout() lays
the area out again.

\section guide_panels Panels

\code
Fl_Dockable_Panel *p = new Fl_Dockable_Panel(area, 220, 400, "Files", files);
p->begin_body();
Fl_Browser *b = new Fl_Browser(0, 0, 220, 400);
p->end_body();
p->body()->resizable(b);
\endcode

A panel is a title bar above a body; widgets made between
Fl_Dockable_Panel::begin_body() and end_body() go in the body, placed
relative to its corner.  A window the application built may be adopted
instead: Fl_Dockable_Panel::adopt() moves its widgets into the body.  A panel
made without a place gets one of its own, named by its title.  Its name, the
title unless Fl_Dockable_Panel::name() sets it, is the key its sizes are
saved under.  Fl_Dockable_Panel::title() changes what its title bar, tab,
stripe button, window and the panel menu show.

Fl_Dockable_Panel::show_panel() shows a panel as its place says, and
hide_panel() hides it.  An application moves it with dock_panel(),
dock_on(), float_panel() and float_as(); each tells the area's callback, as
the user's moves do.  Fl_Dockable_Panel::docked() and panel_shown() say where
it is.

\section guide_requests The application's own code

The user asks for a panel to be opened, hidden or closed; the panel asks the
application through virtual functions an application overrides:

\code
class Notes_Panel : public Fl_Dockable_Panel {
  void close_request() override {
    if (fl_choice("Close the notes?", "Keep", "Close", 0) == 1) hide_panel();
  }
};
\endcode

  - Fl_Dockable_Panel::close_request(): the close button, its window's close
    button, or the title bar's menu.
  - Fl_Dockable_Panel::open_request(): a stripe button, the panel menu, a
    shortcut, or a layout being reset or shown.
  - Fl_Dockable_Panel::hide_request(): a stripe button, the panel menu, a
    shortcut, or the area hiding it.
  - Fl_Dockable_Panel::body_resized(): after the body changes size.

The defaults show or hide the panel.  What the user may do with a panel is
set by Fl_Dockable_Panel::dock_flags(), Fl_Dockable_Flags or'ed together:
closing, floating, and moving it.

\section guide_stripes Stripes and auto-hide

A panel whose place has a stripe has a button on it, which shows and hides
the panel; Fl_Dockable_Panel::show_button() gives it one or takes it away,
and Fl_Dockable_Panel::dock_icon() puts an icon on it.  An auto-hidden panel,
Fl_Dockable_Panel::auto_hide(), slides out from its button over the center
and away again when the user clicks elsewhere or presses Escape;
Fl_Dockable_Panel::pin() puts it back where it was.
Fl_Dockable_Area::stripe_options() sets how the stripes behave: a click on
the button of a panel shown without the keyboard focus can give it the focus
first, and empty stripes can be shown, as places to drop panels.

\section guide_keys Keys and menus

Fl_Dockable_Panel::dock_shortcut() sets a key that shows and hides a panel
from any of the application's windows.  Fl_Dockable_Area::panel_menu() lists
every panel in a menu, each checked while it is shown.
Fl_Dockable_Area::maximize() gives one docked place the whole area, and
Fl_Dockable_Area::hide_docked() hides every docked panel so the center has
the whole area, or shows them again.

\section guide_callback What the user did

The area's callback is called for what the user does, with
Fl::callback_reason() set and Fl_Dockable_Area::callback_panel() returning
the panel, or 0:

  - FL_REASON_DRAGGED: the user moved a panel, docked it, floated it, gave it
    a button or took it away, auto-hid or pinned it.
  - FL_REASON_CLOSED: the user hid a panel.
  - FL_REASON_OPENED: the user showed a panel.
  - FL_REASON_CHANGED: the user resized something, or maximized a panel or
    put it back.

An application that saves its layout as it changes saves on every call.

\section guide_layouts Layouts

\code
area->save_defaults();                      // the default layout, for reset_layout()
Fl_Preferences prefs(Fl_Preferences::USER_L, "example.org", "example");
if (!area->load_layout(prefs)) files->shown = true;
area->callback(save_cb);
win->show();
area->show_saved();
\endcode

  - Fl_Dockable_Area::save_defaults() keeps the places as the default, and
    reset_layout() brings it back.
  - save_layout() and load_layout() write and read every place in an
    Fl_Preferences group; with a name, a layout of that name in it.
    load_layout() is called before the panels are first shown, and
    show_saved() shows those shown when it was saved.
  - write_layout() and read_layout() do the same with text.
  - Values out of range keep the place's own, so a damaged layout cannot put
    a panel out of reach.

A layout may name a place whose panel the application has not made, as for
panels made only when first needed.  Fl_Dockable_Area::panel_factory() sets
the function that makes it when Fl_Dockable_Area::show_place() or a layout
first shows the place.

\section guide_events Events

The area puts a function in front of Fl::event_dispatch(), and takes it away
when the last area is deleted.  An application that sets its own event
dispatch after making an area must call Fl_Dockable_Area::dispatch() from it.

\section guide_lifetime Lifetime

The area deletes the panels docked in it with itself.  A panel floating or
hidden is the application's to delete, and after the area is gone it does
nothing but hide.  Deleting a panel takes it out of the area.

\section guide_dumps Drawing a panel alone

Fl_Dockable_Panel::lend_body() lends the body's widgets to another group,
such as the window the application built them in, placed as they were built,
so the application can draw them there alone, as for a screen dump;
take_back_body() takes them back.

*/
